## Not So Average After All

“Hey, Matt. You’re a data guy, right?” I shrugged and nodded at the same time, acknowledging that I *am* a data guy, but I was reluctant to allow myself to be reduced to such a singular description. “What’s the average net worth of an American family?” Having recently read the Federal Reserve’s 2022 release of its *Survey of Consumer Finances*, I responded, “The *average* net worth was a little more than $1 million in 2022, but…” Before I could qualify my answer, Martin, a colleague with whom several others and I were having lunch, contorted his face into a look representing some mixture of confusion, disbelief, and apprehension as he exclaimed, “What!?! There’s no way that’s true!” I calmly finished my statement by saying, “…the *median* net worth was only about $192,000.” 

Martin looked relieved. He was about five years away from retirement, and didn’t mind telling us that a rough estimate of his own net worth comfortably exceeded that figure. 

“So, I’m above average! Not bad!” he declared. 

"Well, actually..." Not wanting to come across as pedantic or fastidious, I left it there.

I’ve had many similar conversations over the course of my career as a data scientist, and I’ve found that people have a strong desire or need to summarize and compress information into a single value, particularly for purposes of comparison. It’s just easier to reason about a problem when we can compare \( x \) to \( y \) and see which one is larger or smaller. Our brains summarize automatically, and we can only hold a small handful of items in our short-term memory. Psychologists once put the number at seven, plus or minus two, but the modern estimate is closer to just four.<Note>George A. Miller, “The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two: Some Limits on Our Capacity for Processing Information,” <em>Psychological Review</em> 63, no. 2 (1956): 81–97; Nelson Cowan, “The Magical Number 4 in Short-Term Memory: A Reconsideration of Mental Storage Capacity,” <em>Behavioral and Brain Sciences</em> 24, no. 1 (2001): 87–114.</Note> Either way, it’s not a lot. Given this constraint, it makes sense for us to condense information into a single value to not overload our minds as we work through a problem. 

The trouble with Martin’s question is that wealth is not normally distributed. Around 7.5% of families have negative net worth, most have a relatively small net worth, while a tiny fraction of American families are worth billions of dollars. If we plot an estimate of the wealth distribution for American families using the Fed’s 2022 data, as well as the average (mean) and median values, it looks like this:

<Density
	id="net-worth"
	data={netWorth}
	x="x"
	y="density"
	below="share_below"
	xLabel="Net worth"
	xFormat="$.3~s"
	markers={[{ x: s.median, label: 'Median' }, { x: s.mean, label: 'Mean' }]}
	title="Net Worth of U.S. Families, 2022"
	source="Federal Reserve, Survey of Consumer Finances"
	period="2022"
	note="Families worth more than $2.5 million extend off the right edge"
/>

The line in Figure 1.1 shows how common net worths near each value on the horizontal axis are. Another way to think about it is that the area under the curve between two points on the horizontal axis represents the share of families whose net worth lies within that range. 

Notice that the plot is cut off at a value of $2.5 million. The wealthiest family in the survey's public data was worth approximately $2.4 billion. To show that value on a chart with a linear scale in a book would require *very* wide pages. In fact, if the distance from $0 to the $2.5 million mark measures four inches on your page, the $2.4 billion mark would lie 320 feet away --- more than the length of a football field! 

Observe also that the difference between the median and the mean is around $867,000. This shows the importance of not conflating the concepts of mean and median, as Martin did in the conversation above.

So what do we mean, then, when we ask about the average? What we probably want to know is what's *typical* or *most common*, and that's often very different from the statistical mean. Looking at the distribution of net worth in Figure 1.1, many of us might believe some value close to the peak is most representative of an American family's net worth. In that case, we might consider the *mode* of the distribution, or the most frequently occurring value. In this case, the mode is just a few thousand dollars. 

There's a problem with this, though. About six out of every seven families have a net worth that exceeds the mode. Does it make sense to call it the most representative value when it sits below almost everyone? 

This is precisely the challenge that arises when trying to characterize non-normally distributed data with a single summary statistic. Take a moment to think about the median and mean values in Figure 1.1. Does either of those values seem better suited than the other to characterize the *typical* American family's net worth?

Maybe the problem is that for certain distributions, the very idea of a characteristic, typical, usual, or common value simply doesn't apply. What do we do in these cases? These questions and ideas, among others, are what we will explore throughout the rest of the book. 

Before we begin digging into these issues, let's take a look at how normally distributed data arise, and why simple averages do a good job of summarizing them.

## True or False

There are many processes that can be measured with a simple binary outcome: either true or false, yes or no, success or failure. Businesses market to customers via email campaigns and a customer either opens the email, or doesn't. Website visitors either make a purchase, or they leave without buying anything. In quality control processes, a product passes a quality test, or it fails. Applications for credit are approved or they are denied. Soccer players (or footballers, depending on where you are in the world) taking penalty kicks either score, or they don't.

Statisticians call these kinds of processes *Bernoulli trials*, named after the great 17th-century Swiss mathematician, Jacob Bernoulli. If we record each success as a \( 1 \), and each failure as a \( 0 \), the average is nothing more than the proportion of successes, calculated by taking the sum and dividing it by the total number of trials. As the number of trials increases, that proportion settles towards the true probability of success, often represented as \( p \).

To see this in action, let's consider a soccer player taking penalty kicks. As we've already established, each attempt is a Bernoulli trial and it results in either a goal being scored, or no goal. Let's assume that the true, underlying probability of this player scoring on any given attempt is 75%. In the top half of Figure 1.2, we see the running calculation of the player's average, which we can call \( \hat{p} \) (pronounced *p-hat*), and how it evolves as the number of penalty kicks taken increases.

After the first three kicks, \( \hat{p} \) is 100%, and then it slumps down to 62% after 26 kicks before trending upwards towards \( p \) as the number of attempts increases. 

In the bottom half, we see how \( \hat{p} \) evolves over a much larger number of trials, and we can see that the average success rate stabilizes after a couple of hundred measurements and remains tantalizingly close to \( p \), the true probability of success. 

Notice the gray-shaded region on the left-hand side of this plot. The average success rate is very unstable here and tends to swing dramatically as more attempts are added into the mix. This is a sample size effect. If we estimate the probability of success using a small sample, we end up with very different results. As sample sizes get bigger and bigger, our results begin to settle down and calculations from one large sample don't differ much from the others. This is important, and it leads to an intriguing outcome.

<RunningAverage
	id="kicks"
	data={kicks}
	p={k.p}
	strip={40}
	unit="kicks"
	successLabel="Scored"
	failureLabel="Missed"
	notes={[{ x: 26, text: 'a slump: 62% after 26 kicks', dx: 18, dy: 22 }]}
	title="The Share of Goals Settles Toward p"
	source="Simulated"
	note="1,000 penalty kicks by a player who scores 75% of the time"
/>

## Buckets of Success

Suppose we take some number of penalty kick outcomes and put them into a bucket, the size of which we'll just represent as \( n \). As we just established, the success rates for buckets with small \( n \) will tend to vary quite a bit more from bucket to bucket than those with large \( n \). With \( n = 1 \), for example, there are only two possible values a bucket can have: 100% or 0%. If \( n = 2 \), a bucket's success rate can be 0%, 50%, or 100%. As \( n \) increases, the number of possible success rates increases. 

Consider the following experiment. We use buckets that contain either 1, 12, 40, or 100 kicks. For each bucket size, we use 10,000 separate buckets. In other words, we have 10,000 buckets containing a single penalty kick outcome, another 10,000 buckets that each contain the outcomes from a dozen kicks, and so on.

For our \( n = 1 \) buckets (remember, each of these just tells us whether a single penalty kick resulted in a goal), we observe that 24.7% of the kicks were unsuccessful and 75.3% produced a goal. We see this in the top-left panel of Figure 1.3. There are two bars in this simple histogram, one for each outcome (either 0% or 100%) and the height of each bar corresponds to the percentage of the 10,000 buckets that yielded the corresponding outcome. 

In the top-right panel, we have a similar plot, just with more bars since there are more possible outcomes with the larger bucket size of 12 kicks. In this plot, only 4% of buckets had a success rate of 50%, while the most common outcome (26.4% of buckets) was a success rates of 75%. 

<HistogramPanels
	id="buckets"
	data={buckets}
	panel="size"
	x="share"
	y="freq"
	curve={bucketCurve}
	panelTitle={(n) => `Buckets of ${n} ${n === 1 ? 'kick' : 'kicks'}`}
	xDomain={[0, 1]}
	xTicks={[0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]}
	xFormat=".0%"
	yFormat=".1%"
	reference={k.p}
	referenceLabel="p"
	yName="Share of buckets"
	xLabel="Share of goals in the bucket"
	title="As Buckets Grow, the Shares Bunch Up Into a Bell"
	source="Simulated"
	note="10,000 buckets of each size; each panel has its own vertical scale"
/>

Moving down to the bottom row, we see a very exciting shape has emerged with our \( n = 40 \) buckets. The largest share of the 10,000 buckets (15%) had a success rate of 75%, and all of our buckets tend to cluster around that figure, approaching a shape that looks like a normal distribution! The final histogram calculated from the 10,000 buckets of 100 kicks shows the same phenomenon as its bars conform to the smooth curve of the normal distribution even more closely.

So what does this mean? Can we summarize our soccer player's penalty kick success rate (across many sets of 100 kicks) using a single number?

Yes, and Figure 1.4 shows why. It depicts a normal distribution of penalty kick success rates with a mean success rate of 75% and a standard deviation of 4.3 percentage points. We haven't discussed standard deviations yet, so for now, just think of the standard deviation as how spread out the distribution is. Large standard deviations yield shorter, wider distributions while smaller standard deviations produce taller, narrower distributions. 

<Density
	id="bell"
	data={bell}
	x="x"
	y="density"
	xLabel="Share of goals in the bucket"
	xFormat=".0%"
	belowLabel="Share of buckets below"
	markers={[{ x: 'mean' }, { x: 'median' }, { x: 'mode' }]}
	title="In a Normal Distribution, the Three Averages Agree"
	note="Normal curve for buckets of 100 kicks: mean 75%, standard deviation 4.3 points"
/>

This distribution shows us why it's sometimes okay to use the word *average* so loosely. Average is a slippery word, and people using it might be referring to the mean, median, or mode. And when data are distributed normally, that's completely acceptable --- they are in fact all the same value, as we see in Figure 1.4! 

## The Central Idea

Let's step back for a moment and briefly chronicle our journey from simple binary outcomes to a bell curve whose measures of central tendency (mean, median, and mode) are the same. In the top-left panel of Figure 1.3, we saw a histogram of single kick outcomes, and it looked nothing like a bell curve. However, once we started averaging over an increasing number of penalty kicks, those averages started bunching up into a bell shape. 

This isn't a peculiarity of penalty kicks --- it's a mathematical result that statisticians call the *Central Limit Theorem*. If you add up or average enough independent random values, the results tend towards a bell curve as you include more of them, regardless of what the individual values look like. Note that computing a sum and an average are nearly the same thing; one is just divided by the count while the other is not. For example, consider the distribution of possible outcomes when rolling a die. For a single die, the distribution is flat, or uniform, across the discrete values of one to six. However, if you roll ten dice and take the sum, the distribution forms a bell curve (centered on 35 in this case). 

A store's daily sales total tends to form a bell curve, even though individual purchases are very uneven. A typical store might make many small sales in a day and just a few big sales, but because each day's total is the sum of many purchases, the totals look like a normal distribution from one ordinary day to the next. 

## The Fine Print

At this point you may be slightly confused. This chapter opened with the claim that a lot of the real-world data that people care about are *not* distributed normally, but we just saw that the bell curve arises when we take sums or averages of independent random variables, regardless of what they look like individually. So why didn't it work with the net worth distribution?

Before we answer that, let me provide you with a checklist of sorts that we can go through to determine whether we should expect a process to result in a normal distribution:

- **Many pieces** --- Do the values consist of many distinct contributions rather than a few?
- **Additive** --- Does each piece add to the totals rather than multiplying them?
- **Independent** --- Does each piece occur on its own without influence from others?
- **No giants** --- Is every piece small relative to the total?
- **One group** --- Do all the values come from one group as opposed to a mix of groups with different central tendencies?

If the answer to each of these questions is *yes*, we would expect a single peak, roughly symmetrical around the center, and very few values that are far from the mean. If the answer to any of the above questions is *no*, our bell will either warp, fracture, or never form at all.

Returning to our question, wealth fails several items on our checklist. Most importantly, it isn't built by adding up many small pieces like our penalty kicks. Wealth grows through a multiplicative process, since returns compound and tomorrow's gain depends on today's value. This also touches on the independence test. Advantages tend to build on one another, so families that start with a large inheritance, for example, will build even more wealth over time. The Fed's survey also mixes families at very different stages of life, from young couples who have only just begun to save to retirees near their lifetime peak, so the data fail our one-group test as well.

